2098:- next-step recommendation: `stmap38` shows that a wider first-pass root/fanin/fanout hotspot neighborhood still misses the tracked `syn2` strong-seed candidate at AIG ID `2318`, despite selecting the maximum `128` hotspots and seeing aggregate SCL feedback severity `0.884`. For `stmap39`, switch from static AIG-neighborhood matching to a reconstruction-aware association: map each final candidate cut or reconstructed supergate output to the first-pass mapped node IDs that consume its fanout, then penalize strong seeds by downstream consumers' measured SCL load ratio rather than by nearby original AIG IDs alone.

codex
The stmap39 feedback lookup can attach consumer pressure to the wrong mapper nodes because it mixes mapper node numbers with a table of AIG IDs. The remaining issue affects the accuracy of the new diagnostic ranking.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Do not look up mapper numbers in the AIG-id table — /Users/cunxiy/Documents/research/autoresearch-eda/abc-harness/src/map/mapper/mapperMatch.c:942-942
  When `Map_NodeReadAigId(pNode)` is not found, this fallback looks up `pNode->Num` in the same pressure array, but the command populates that array with `LoadStats.HotspotAigIds`, not node numbers. In mode 40 this can apply the SCL pressure penalty to unrelated candidates whose internal mapper number happens to equal a hot AIG id, while still failing to match the collected mapped-node ids.

- [P3] Re-sort hotspots after updating an existing ratio — /Users/cunxiy/Documents/research/autoresearch-eda/abc-harness/src/base/abci/abcStmap_39.c:120-124
  When the same AIG hotspot is revisited with a larger ratio, these lines update the ratio in place and return without restoring the descending order. Since `Abc_Stmap39PrintHotspots` reports the first entries as ranked consumer-pressure hotspots, later fanin/fanout paths can make the diagnostics and parsed top-hotspot CSV omit or mis-rank the actual hottest consumers.
